From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 01:21:51 +0300
Subject: ES_LOW_MEMORY: bound the decoded images waiting for upload, free
 frames before the atlas upload, NV30_FORCE_DXT=all

The first PS3 run ran out of memory while loading (kernel OOM at 125 s, "out of VRAM" from
ps3rsx from 30 s on). Two causes:
- SpriteLoadManager started 100 load chains. The workers decode faster than the main thread
  uploads, so up to 100 decoded images waited in RAM (+195 MB on the PC). Six chains keep two
  workers busy.
- Nothing set NV30_FORCE_DXT, so the power-of-two RGBA8 atlases (about 380 MB) went to VRAM
  uncompressed and overflowed it. main() now defaults it to "all" (Mesa 0038: DXT5, a quarter;
  other drivers ignore it). The atlas upload must stay one glTexImage2D with data: the
  policy skips glTexImage2D(NULL) and keeps the texture uncompressed for sub-image updates.
- The decoded frames are freed before the grid is handed to the driver.

diff --git a/source/image/Sprite.cpp b/source/image/Sprite.cpp
--- a/source/image/Sprite.cpp
+++ b/source/image/Sprite.cpp
@@ -149,6 +149,9 @@ namespace {
 					for(int row = 0; row < h; ++row)
 						copy_n(buffer.Begin(row, frame), w, grid.begin() + (y + row) * layout.width + x);
 				}
+				// The decoded frames are not needed any more: free them before the driver copies
+				// or compresses the grid, so that the two never coexist.
+				buffer.Clear();
 				glTexImage2D(type, 0, GL_RGBA8, layout.width, layout.height, 0,
 					GL_RGBA, GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE, grid.data());
 			}
diff --git a/source/image/SpriteLoadManager.cpp b/source/image/SpriteLoadManager.cpp
--- a/source/image/SpriteLoadManager.cpp
+++ b/source/image/SpriteLoadManager.cpp
@@ -156,7 +156,15 @@ void SpriteLoadManager::Init(TaskQueue &queue, map<string, shared_ptr<ImageSet>>
 	// of tasks the main thread can handle in a single frame to limit peak memory usage.
 	{
 		lock_guard lock(imageQueueMutex);
-		for(int i = 0; i < TaskQueue::MAX_SYNC_TASKS; ++i)
+#ifdef ES_LOW_MEMORY
+		// Every chain holds a decoded image from the worker thread until the main thread has uploaded
+		// it, and the workers are faster than the main thread: with 100 chains the decoded images
+		// pile up (200 MB on the PC, more than a PS3 has). A few more than the workers keeps them busy.
+		const int chains = 6;
+#else
+		const int chains = TaskQueue::MAX_SYNC_TASKS;
+#endif
+		for(int i = 0; i < chains; ++i)
 			LoadSpriteQueued(queue);
 	}
 }
diff --git a/source/main.cpp b/source/main.cpp
--- a/source/main.cpp
+++ b/source/main.cpp
@@ -80,6 +80,9 @@ this program. If not, see <https://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
 #include <fstream>
 #include <unistd.h>
 #endif
+#ifdef ES_LOW_MEMORY
+#include <cstdlib>
+#endif
 #if defined(ES_LOW_MEMORY) && defined(__GLIBC__)
 #include <malloc.h>
 #endif
@@ -106,6 +109,12 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 	mallopt(M_MMAP_THRESHOLD, 128 * 1024);
 	mallopt(M_ARENA_MAX, 2);
 #endif
+#ifdef ES_LOW_MEMORY
+	// On the PS3 the sprite atlases (RGBA8, power-of-two) are bigger than its video memory; Mesa's
+	// texture compression at upload (docs/MEMORY.md) stores them as DXT5, a quarter of that. Other
+	// drivers ignore the variable. NV30_FORCE_DXT=off in the environment turns it off.
+	setenv("NV30_FORCE_DXT", "all", 0);
+#endif
 #ifdef _WIN32
 	WinVersion::Init();
 	// Handle command-line arguments
